## III. When to Stop Testing

Testing should be stopped when:

- All critical and major defects are resolved and verified.
- Test coverage meets the requirements and all test cases are executed.
- The product meets acceptance criteria defined by stakeholders.
- Remaining issues are low-priority and do not block release.
- The risk of releasing with known issues is acceptable to the business.
- The project timeline or budget constraints require release.

However, testing is never truly "complete"—it is often suspended based on risk, priorities, and resource availability.
